        "classification_retained_from_prior_manual_review": "canonical_identity_match"
      },
      "current": {
        "id": 21837,
        "is_similar": false,
        "description": "<p>This is a randomized two-arm pilot study comparing dogs with advanced melanoma treated with nanoparticle calreticulin based immunotherapy combined with a focused ultrasound (HIFU) with or without an immune checkpoint inhibitor (anti-PDL-1). We have previously demonstrated that delivering a calreticulin (CRT) expression plasmid via a cationic liposome nanoparticle (CRT-NP) generates an immunostimulatory signal in mice tumors, akin to the immunogenic cell death-associated external cell membrane-bound CRT induced by radiation. CRT is a well-known multifunctional chaperone protein involved in cellular calcium homeostasis and immune modulation. The primary aim of this study is to evaluate local and systemic (abscopal) immune effects of CRT-NP based immunotherapy in combination with HIFU with and without an anti-PDL-1 monoclonal antibody (immune checkpoint inhibitor). Dogs will receive CRT-NP intratumorally once weekly for 4-8 weeks along with HIFU applied weekly to the same tumors. An anti-PDL-1 monoclonal antibody will be administered intravenously every 2 weeks, three days post HIFU treatment. Dogs will undergo baseline comprehensive lab work and urinalysis, which will also be monitored throughout treatment. A baseline CT scan or radiographs will be performed and repeated 12 weeks after the start of treatment to monitor for metastasis/progression of disease. Tumor volume will be measured at each visit along with adverse event reporting at each visit as well according to the VCOG Criteria for Adverse Events v. 2020. Tumor biopsies will be performed to monitor immune modulation, T cell infiltration, myeloid markers, and cytokine response. Dogs with a durable response will be monitored long term every 2-3 months. This study has been approved by the UTSW Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee.</p>",
        "absolute_url": "https://veterinaryclinicaltrials.org/s/nanoparticle-crt-combined-with-high-frequency-ultrasound-with-or-without-immune-checkpoint-inhibitors-in-dogs-with-advanced-melanoma-203183/?ref=gallery",
        "tagline": "Immunotherapy and High Frequency Ultrasound in Dogs with Advanced Melanoma",
        "name": "Nanoparticle CRT Combined with High Frequency Ultrasound with or without Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors in Dogs with Advanced Melanoma",
